Z||000000||Motorhead||07-29-2001||05:12 AM||||This is a new one on me.<BR>My 2.5 has begun blowing vast quantities of oil out any opening it can find.  The crankcase is being pressurized somehow.  I removed the head and had it checked, but there were no cracks or warpage.  Next step is to tear down the block.  I'm guessing either a cracked block or bad rings.<BR>The car overheated recently due to a broken belt.  I attribute this new problem to my own stupidity by allowing that to happen. <BR>Anyone have any further thoughts?<BR>Thanks,<BR>Mike <BR><P>------------------<BR>"The one who dies with the most toys wins!"<BR>  bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1372350&a=10234998&p=38686771&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||87GTZ34||07-29-2001||10:32 AM||||Usually caused by bad rings.  Did it have some 'blow-by' before the belt incident?  I'm curious how just one cooking could cause that much change unless it actually broke a ring.  BTW In my opinion overheating is one of the worst things that can happen to an engine as it takes a lot of life from them. When I buy a used car, I always ask the person selling how many times it was overheated and get the same answer... 'none'.  On my 2nd Fiero, the guy drove it so much with a cooling system problem that he had to have the motor rebuilt.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Motorhead||07-29-2001||01:08 PM||||This engine never leaked or used any oil between changes before the overheating.  That's why I'm kicking myself in the butt for letting it happen.<BR>I'm pulling the engine this week, but with the head removed there's no evidence of a broken ring on the top side of the pistons.  Although #4 appears wet with oil, but only slightly.  Cylinder walls look good.  <BR>Thanks for the input.<P>Mike<BR><P>------------------<BR>"The one who dies with the most toys wins!"<BR>  bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-29-2001||03:03 PM||||I'd have to go with a cooked ring. They ususally don't break from an over heat. They simply loose their tension and don't seal.||||reg||1
